# Vimla Design System

These rules are mandatory for all Vimla frontend UI work.

The goal is to maintain one coherent, reusable production design system across:

- `apps/web`
- `apps/admin`
- future frontend applications

Do not build pages as isolated collections of custom styles.

Vimla must use reusable primitives, semantic design tokens, consistent typography, motion, accessibility, and interaction patterns.

The core implementation principle is:

**Reuse first → compose second → extend third → create new only when necessary.**

---

# 1. Visual direction

Vimla should feel:

- clean;
- minimal;
- calm;
- modern;
- lightweight;
- premium without being flashy;
- easy to understand without training;
- AI-native;
- collaborative;
- motion-aware.

Vimla may take inspiration from products such as ChatGPT, Linear, Notion, and Arc in terms of simplicity and clarity, but it must NOT clone their UI.

Prefer:

- generous whitespace;
- restrained use of color;
- subtle borders;
- clear visual hierarchy;
- predictable controls;
- calm states;
- subtle motion;
- strong readability.

Avoid unless explicitly justified:

- excessive gradients;
- heavy glassmorphism;
- cyberpunk/gaming aesthetics;
- oversized shadows;
- decorative UI that harms clarity;
- turning every piece of content into a large card;
- inconsistent page-specific styling.

---

# 2. Sources of truth

Once established, the authoritative design sources are:

```text
docs/DESIGN_SYSTEM.md
packages/ui
```

Visual references should live under:

```text
docs/design/
```

For example:

```text
docs/design/vimla-ui-reference.png
```

Reference images define visual direction only.

They are NOT exact runtime CSS specifications.

If a generated reference contains:

- inconsistent spacing;
- malformed text;
- fake icons;
- impossible UI states;
- accessibility issues;
- contradictory component states;

do not reproduce those mistakes literally.

The coded design system is the final implementation source of truth.

---

# 3. Shared UI package

All reusable UI primitives belong in:

```text
packages/ui
```

Package name:

```text
@vimla/ui
```

It should be reused by at least:

```text
apps/web
apps/admin
```

Do NOT create an independent design system for Admin.

Admin may use denser layouts, but should reuse the same:

- tokens;
- typography;
- buttons;
- inputs;
- dialogs;
- badges;
- tabs;
- tables;
- focus states;
- theme system;
- motion system.

---

# 4. Search before creating components

Before implementing any reusable UI element:

1. search `@vimla/ui`;
2. reuse an existing component if possible;
3. extend an existing component if the use case is repeatable;
4. create a new shared primitive only if no suitable abstraction exists.

Do NOT create page-specific primitives such as:

```text
LoginButton
SettingsButton
BillingButton
ProjectButton
```

when they are simply different uses of the same shared Button.

Use composition:

```tsx
<Button variant="primary">
  ...
</Button>
```

---

# 5. Shared primitives

The following should normally come from `@vimla/ui`:

```text
Button
IconButton

Input
PasswordInput
SearchInput
Textarea
OtpInput

FormField

Checkbox
Radio
Switch

Select
DropdownMenu
SegmentedControl
Tabs

Dialog
Modal
Drawer
Popover
Tooltip

Toast

Badge
StatusBadge

Avatar
AvatarGroup

Progress
UsageMeter

Card

Skeleton
Spinner

EmptyState
ErrorState
Alert
Divider
```

If one of these is missing, first consider adding it to `@vimla/ui` instead of creating a local implementation.

---

# 6. Product-level components

Product-level components are allowed when they represent real Vimla concepts.

Examples:

```text
ConversationItem
ProjectListItem
PlanCard
UsageSummary
PaymentStatus
MemberListItem
StreamingIndicator
AiModeSelector
```

However, product-level components should still compose shared primitives.

For example, `PlanCard` should reuse the shared:

```text
Button
Badge
Text
```

rather than inventing new button or badge styles.

---

# 7. Feature styles are mainly layout

Feature-level SCSS Modules may define:

- grid;
- flex composition;
- page layout;
- positioning;
- feature-specific responsive structure;
- spacing between major sections.

Feature SCSS SHOULD NOT redefine shared visual primitives.

Do not redefine locally:

- button appearance;
- input appearance;
- modal appearance;
- checkbox appearance;
- badges;
- toasts;
- focus rings;
- global typography;
- common shadows;
- global radius system;
- global colors.

If shared visual behavior is missing, extend `@vimla/ui`.

---

# 8. Design tokens

All global design values must come from centralized semantic tokens.

Do not scatter raw visual values throughout application code.

Use semantic CSS custom properties such as:

```text
--vimla-bg
--vimla-surface
--vimla-surface-secondary
--vimla-surface-hover

--vimla-text
--vimla-text-secondary
--vimla-text-muted

--vimla-border
--vimla-border-strong

--vimla-accent
--vimla-accent-hover
--vimla-accent-active
--vimla-accent-soft

--vimla-success
--vimla-warning
--vimla-danger

--vimla-focus-ring
```

Prefer semantic naming over component-specific or raw color naming.

GOOD:

```scss
background: var(--vimla-surface);
color: var(--vimla-text);
border-color: var(--vimla-border);
```

BAD:

```scss
background: #ffffff;
color: #0f172a;
border-color: #cbd5e1;
```

---

# 9. Initial visual palette

The initial Vimla visual direction uses an indigo-based accent family.

Reference values:

```text
Accent
#6366F1

Accent Light
#A5B4FC

Accent Soft
#E0E7FF

Gray 900
#0F172A

Gray 600
#475569

Gray 300
#CBD5E1

Gray 100
#F1F5F9

Success
#10B981

Warning
#F59E0B

Danger
#EF4444
```

These values should live only in the token/theme implementation.

Components consume semantic tokens.

Interaction colors may be adjusted for:

- contrast;
- hover;
- active;
- focus;
- disabled;
- dark mode.

Do not casually change Vimla's overall visual identity.

---

# 10. No raw colors in feature code

Outside the theme/token implementation, do not hardcode ordinary product colors using:

```text
#xxxxxx
rgb()
rgba()
hsl()
```

unless the exception is genuinely necessary and documented.

BAD:

```scss
background: #6366f1;
```

GOOD:

```scss
background: var(--vimla-accent);
```

---

# 11. Light, dark, and system appearance

All reusable components must support:

```text
light
dark
system
```

through semantic tokens.

Do not implement independent dark-mode color logic in every feature component.

The theme layer owns token mappings.

Feature components should usually not care which theme is active.

---

# 12. Typography

Typography must be centralized.

Do not invent arbitrary font sizes on individual pages.

Use semantic typography concepts such as:

```text
Page title
Section title
Subsection title
Body
Secondary
Caption
Label
Meta
```

Initial direction:

```text
Page title
32 / 40
Bold

Section title
20 / 28
Semibold

Body
16 / 24
Regular

Secondary
14 / 20
Regular

Caption / Meta
12–13 px
```

Exact values belong in the design system.

Prefer reusable typography primitives such as:

```text
Heading
Text
```

or centralized typography classes/tokens.

---

# 13. Spacing

Use one centralized spacing scale.

Initial scale:

```text
4
8
12
16
24
32
40
48
64
```

Avoid arbitrary values such as:

```text
13px
17px
19px
23px
```

when the layout can be represented by existing spacing tokens.

---

# 14. Border radius

Use a centralized radius scale.

Initial direction:

```text
8
10
12
14
```

mapped to semantic names such as:

```text
sm
md
lg
xl
round
```

Do not define a unique radius system per page.

---

# 15. Shadows and elevation

Shadows should be centralized and restrained.

Prefer:

- borders;
- surface contrast;
- spacing;

before adding shadows.

Do not add heavy shadows to every card.

If elevation levels are needed, define semantic elevation tokens in the design system.

---

# 16. Motion

Motion is part of Vimla's visual identity.

Animations must be:

- subtle;
- fast;
- functional;
- consistent;
- non-blocking.

Use centralized motion tokens.

Typical timing ranges:

```text
fast
120–150ms

normal
180–220ms

slow
250–300ms
```

Examples of appropriate motion:

- button hover/press;
- sidebar collapse;
- dropdown appearance;
- modal fade + scale;
- toast slide/fade;
- list item insertion;
- AI streaming indicator;
- Project Brain pulse.

Avoid:

- bouncing;
- long decorative transitions;
- motion that delays user interaction.

---

# 17. Reduced motion

Always respect:

```css
prefers-reduced-motion
```

Animations must never be essential for understanding or operating the UI.

---

# 18. Buttons

Use the shared `Button`.

Core variants:

```text
primary
secondary
ghost
destructive
```

Core sizes:

```text
sm
md
lg
```

Required states:

```text
default
hover
active
focus-visible
disabled
loading
```

Do not create separate CSS implementations for buttons on Sign In, Settings, Billing, Projects, or Admin pages.

Loading state should not unexpectedly change button width.

---

# 19. Inputs

Use shared:

```text
Input
PasswordInput
SearchInput
Textarea
OtpInput
```

Inputs should share consistent:

- height;
- padding;
- typography;
- border;
- radius;
- focus state;
- invalid state;
- disabled state.

Do not create a separate visual input implementation for each page.

---

# 20. FormField

Forms should compose a shared `FormField` pattern.

Example:

```tsx
<FormField
  label={...}
  description={...}
  error={...}
>
  <Input />
</FormField>
```

Do not repeatedly implement custom:

```text
label
input
description
validation error
```

markup on every screen.

Auth, Settings, Billing, and Admin forms should use the same visual form language.

---

# 21. Validation

Validation errors must be shown through Vimla UI.

Do not rely on browser-native validation bubbles as the primary UX.

Errors should:

- be localized;
- be understandable;
- use stable app semantics;
- visually follow the shared design system.

---

# 22. Dialogs and modals

Use one shared Dialog/Modal system.

It should support:

- title;
- description;
- body content;
- actions;
- close;
- destructive state;
- keyboard navigation;
- focus trap;
- Escape handling.

Do not create a separate modal implementation for each feature.

---

# 23. Toasts

Use one shared toast/notification system.

Core variants:

```text
success
info
warning
error
```

Do not create separate notification systems for Auth, Billing, Settings, or Admin.

---

# 24. Badges

Use a shared Badge system.

Core variants:

```text
neutral
accent
success
warning
danger
locked
```

Examples:

```text
AI
PRO
Free
Active
Locked
```

Do not create page-specific badge styling.

---

# 25. Icons

Use one consistent icon system/library across Vimla.

Do not casually mix:

```text
Lucide
Heroicons
Material Icons
random inline SVG libraries
emoji
```

Do not use emoji as normal production UI icons.

Custom brand/product icons are allowed when intentional.

---

# 26. App Shell

Application navigation should reuse shared shell components.

Prefer concepts such as:

```text
AppShell
Sidebar
SidebarSection
SidebarItem
SidebarFooter
```

Do not rebuild independent sidebars for:

```text
Chat
Projects
Settings
```

Desktop:

```text
expanded
collapsed
```

Mobile:

```text
Drawer / Sheet
```

---

# 27. Authentication UI

All authentication screens must share one visual architecture.

Applies to:

```text
Sign In
Sign Up
Verify Email
Forgot Password
Reset Password
```

Prefer shared:

```text
AuthLayout
AuthCard
FormField
Input
PasswordInput
OtpInput
Button
Alert
```

Do not create six unrelated auth designs.

---

# 28. Settings UI

Settings pages should share:

```text
SettingsLayout
SettingsNavigation
SettingsSection
```

Applies to:

```text
Account
Appearance
Language
Security
Billing
Usage
Notifications
```

Do not build an independent section design for each Settings page.

---

# 29. Chat UI

Chat-specific components should be reusable.

Prefer shared concepts such as:

```text
ChatComposer
Message
AssistantMessage
UserMessage
StreamingIndicator
ModelSelector
ConversationItem
```

Normal chat and future Project chat should reuse the same lower-level primitives where appropriate.

---

# 30. AUTO / PRO product semantics

Do not change AI-mode product semantics during styling work.

AUTO:

- shows the selected Auto level;
- does NOT show the hidden underlying routed model.

PRO:

- shows the concrete model manually selected by the user.

UI must never reveal hidden Auto routing.

---

# 31. Usage UI

Normal users should see understandable Usage, not internal provider economics.

Prefer reusable:

```text
UsageMeter
UsageSummary
```

Do not expose normal users to:

```text
providerActualCostMicroRub
internal margin
provider reservation accounting
provider cost internals
```

---

# 32. Admin reuse

`apps/admin` must reuse the same design foundation.

Admin may use denser:

- tables;
- filters;
- forms;
- data layouts.

But the core controls remain shared.

Where useful, components may expose an explicit density:

```text
comfortable
compact
```

Do not create many overlapping variants such as:

```text
small
mini
admin
dense
compactDense
```

without a real need.

---

# 33. Tables

Data-heavy Admin tables should use shared table primitives.

Required states:

- loading;
- empty;
- error;
- selected;
- sortable where applicable;
- pagination/cursor controls.

Do not encode important status only by color.

---

# 34. Responsive behavior

Responsive behavior must be intentional.

Do not simply shrink the desktop UI.

Centralize breakpoints.

Desktop:

- persistent/collapsible sidebar;
- wide workspace.

Mobile:

- drawer/sheet navigation;
- touch-friendly controls;
- adapted composer;
- no accidental horizontal overflow.

Avoid random media-query breakpoints per feature.

---

# 35. Accessibility

All new UI must be accessible by default.

Required:

- semantic HTML;
- correct labels;
- keyboard navigation;
- visible `focus-visible`;
- sufficient contrast;
- appropriate ARIA attributes;
- correct disabled semantics;
- accessible validation/error association;
- dialog focus management;
- Escape handling where expected;
- touch-friendly targets;
- reduced-motion support.

Do not remove browser focus outlines without replacing them with an accessible shared focus ring.

---

# 36. i18n

Generic shared UI components must not hardcode product copy.

Components receive:

```text
label
description
aria-label
children
```

from the consuming application.

Actual product translations remain in next-intl dictionaries.

Dev-only UI catalog examples may contain sample copy.

---

# 37. Business logic separation

UI refactors must NOT rewrite working product/domain/security logic unless explicitly requested.

Do not casually change:

```text
Better Auth
OTP
sessions
billing
T-Bank
Finance
ProxyAPI
AI Gateway
Usage
permissions
Admin security
```

Visual work should compose around existing behavior.

Never break a secure working flow only to match a screenshot.

---

# 38. Inline styles

Avoid ordinary product styling such as:

```tsx
style={{
  color: "...",
  padding: "...",
  borderRadius: "..."
}}
```

Use:

- design tokens;
- shared components;
- SCSS Modules.

Inline styles are acceptable when genuinely data-driven and not representable through a reusable semantic class/token.

---

# 39. Styling technology

The project uses SCSS Modules.

Do NOT add another styling system without an explicit architectural decision.

Do not introduce:

```text
Tailwind
styled-components
Emotion
Bootstrap
Material UI
Chakra
Ant Design
```

for convenience.

Do not replace the Vimla visual system with a complete third-party visual framework.

Headless/accessibility primitives may be considered only when they solve a real implementation problem and do not own Vimla's visual identity.

---

# 40. UI dependencies

Before installing a new UI dependency:

- check whether an existing dependency already solves the problem;
- check whether `@vimla/ui` already provides the primitive;
- verify that the package is actually necessary.

Do not install a library only for one simple button, dropdown, modal, or tooltip if the project already has an equivalent solution.

---

# 41. Composition over prop explosion

Prefer small composable components.

Avoid giant components with many booleans such as:

```text
isSettings
isBilling
isProject
isPurple
isCompact
isChat
isAnotherVariant
```

If a visual variation represents a real repeatable use case, define an explicit typed variant.

Otherwise compose components.

---

# 42. Loading states

Prefer:

- Skeleton;
- inline progress;
- local Spinner;
- StreamingIndicator.

Avoid unnecessary full-screen spinners.

Loading UI must reuse shared components.

---

# 43. Empty states

Use a shared EmptyState language.

Empty states should be:

- concise;
- useful;
- calm;
- action-oriented when appropriate.

Avoid huge random illustrations unless explicitly part of the product direction.

---

# 44. Error states

Use shared:

```text
Alert
ErrorState
```

Show user-friendly copy.

Do not expose raw provider/backend errors as primary UI messages.

Preserve stable backend error semantics.

---

# 45. Destructive actions

Destructive operations should use:

- destructive Button variant;
- clear confirmation;
- concise explanation;
- shared Dialog.

Red should primarily communicate:

- destructive action;
- error.

Do not use red decoratively throughout normal UI.

---

# 46. Development UI catalog

Once the design-system foundation exists, provide a local/test-only component catalog such as:

```text
/dev/ui
```

It should show:

- colors;
- typography;
- Buttons;
- Inputs;
- FormField;
- Badges;
- Avatars;
- Select;
- Dialog;
- Toast;
- UsageMeter;
- AUTO/PRO selector;
- loading states;
- empty states;
- dark mode.

This route must NOT exist in production.

When shared components change, inspect the UI catalog.

---

# 47. Working from screenshots / visual references

When a new UI screenshot/reference is provided:

1. study the composition;
2. map visible elements to existing `@vimla/ui` components;
3. reuse design tokens;
4. create only missing product-level components;
5. use feature styles mainly for layout;
6. do not duplicate Button/Input/Dialog/etc.

A screenshot defines composition and visual direction.

It does NOT authorize creating a miniature new design system inside one page.

---

# 48. New page checklist

Before considering a new UI page complete, verify:

- existing shared components were reused;
- no duplicate Button exists;
- no duplicate Input exists;
- no duplicate Dialog exists;
- no raw colors were introduced;
- typography comes from the system;
- spacing comes from the system;
- radius comes from the system;
- light mode works;
- dark mode works;
- keyboard navigation works;
- focus-visible exists;
- mobile works;
- loading state exists where applicable;
- error state exists where applicable;
- RU/EN works;
- business logic was not unintentionally changed.

---

# 49. New shared component checklist

Before adding a reusable component, verify:

- it represents a true reusable primitive or repeated pattern;
- it has typed props;
- variants are meaningful;
- accessibility is implemented;
- keyboard behavior is correct where applicable;
- focus state exists;
- disabled state exists where applicable;
- light/dark themes work;
- reduced motion is respected;
- it is reusable outside one page;
- it is exported through `@vimla/ui`;
- it is documented in the UI catalog when appropriate.

---

# 50. Duplication checks

During substantial UI work, inspect the repository for duplicated styling/primitives.

Examples:

```bash
rg '#[0-9A-Fa-f]{6}' apps/web apps/admin
rg '<button' apps/web apps/admin
rg '<input' apps/web apps/admin
```

Do not mechanically replace every native element.

Investigate results and verify that shared application primitives are used where appropriate.

---

# 51. Priority order

If a screenshot conflicts with other requirements, use this priority:

1. security;
2. product correctness;
3. accessibility;
4. Vimla Design System consistency;
5. visual similarity to the screenshot.

Never break auth, billing, permissions, or security merely for pixel-perfect matching.

---

# 52. Main rule

Vimla must feel like one coherent product.

Do not design each new screen as a separate mini-application.

Always:

**reuse first, compose second, extend third, create a new primitive only as the last option.**
